---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import SiteHeader from "../components/SiteHeader.astro";
import SiteFooter from "../components/SiteFooter.astro";
import "../styles/global.css";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Help | Time Zone Guru"
  description="How to compare cities, read overlap, handle daylight saving, and edit the sleep/work/awake template."
>
  <SiteHeader />
  <main class="site-main content-page">
    <article class="prose">
      <p class="content-kicker"><a href="/">← Compare</a></p>
      <h1>Help</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        Time Zone Guru shows the same moment in several places at once, so you
        can spot when people are likely free, even when daylight saving rules
        differ.
      </p>

      <h2>Quick start</h2>
      <ol>
        <li>
          <strong>Choose a date</strong> (or tap Today). Rows are hours of
          <em>your</em> local day on that date.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Add cities</strong> with the search box. Your local city is seeded
          automatically when we can detect it.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Read the grid</strong>: left column is you; other columns are
          each city’s wall clock for that same instant.
        </li>
        <li>
          Look for green in the <strong>You</strong> column. That’s when more people
          are awake.
        </li>
      </ol>

      <h2>How to read the schedule</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Rows = hours</strong> of your local day. Scrolling is vertical,
          so it works on a phone.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Columns = locations</strong>. Each cell is that city’s local
          time at the same absolute moment as your hour.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>+1 / −1</strong> badges mean that city is already on the next or
          previous calendar day.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Sleep</strong> cells use the page background so off-hours stay quiet.
          Work and awake stay colored.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>The “You” column (overlap)</h2>
      <p>
        For each of your local hours we count how many selected cities are
        <em>awake</em> (work or free-time “awake” in the activity template). Greener means better
        multi-person availability. The small fraction (e.g. <code>2/3</code>) is
        awake / total cities.
      </p>

      <h2>Daylight saving &amp; offsets</h2>
      <p>
        Times use the browser’s IANA timezone database via <code>Intl</code>.
        When you pick a date in March or November, offsets update automatically
        for zones that observe DST. Melbourne and New York do not switch on the
        same weekend, for example.
      </p>
      <p>
        City names and zone ids come from
        <a href="https://github.com/vvo/tzdb" rel="noopener noreferrer"
          >@vvo/tzdb</a
        >
        (major cities on simplified IANA zones), not from inventing offsets by hand.
      </p>

      <h2>Sleep / work / awake template</h2>
      <p>
        Open <strong>Typical day</strong> at the bottom of the compare tool. That
        template is matched to each city’s <em>local</em> hour (not yours). Defaults
        assume night sleep, daytime work, evening awake, which you edit freely.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>24-hour clock</strong>: midnight at the top, hours clockwise,
          one slice per hour (15°). Drag the ring to paint.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>AM / PM strips</strong>: two rows of twelve hours for precise
          taps.
        </li>
        <li>Presets: workday 09–17, all sleep, all awake, or reset.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Share a comparison</h2>
      <p>
        Under the schedule, use <strong>Copy link</strong> (or <strong>Share…</strong> on
        supported phones). The link encodes selected cities in the URL. Optionally include
        the date and your sleep/work/awake day template. Anyone with the link sees that
        comparison. No account required.
      </p>
      <p>
        Example:
        <code>?z=Europe/London,America/New_York&amp;d=2026-07-15</code>
      </p>

      <h2>Tips</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          Search by city, country, or IANA id (e.g. <code>Tokyo</code>, <code
            >Brazil</code
          >, <code>Europe/Paris</code>).
        </li>
        <li>Remove a city by tapping its chip.</li>
        <li>Toggle 24-hour vs 12-hour labels in the Schedule panel.</li>
      </ul>

      <p>
        More background on why this exists:
        <a href="/about/">About</a>.
      </p>
    </article>
  </main>
  <SiteFooter />
</BaseLayout>
